  "title": "Morgen, Kinder, wird's was geben",
  "composer": "Carl Gottlieb Hering",
  "about": "Morgen, Kinder, wird's was geben means Tomorrow, children, there will be something in German. Karl Friedrich Splittegarb's words were first printed in 1795, under the title Die Weihnachtsfreude. Carl Gottlieb Hering set them to this tune in 1809, in a singing school for children. The song is about children looking forward to their presents, and it never mentions the birth of Jesus.",
  "funFact": "In 1927 Erich Kästner wrote a parody about poor children, which begins Morgen, Kinder, wird's nichts geben: there will be nothing.",
